{:edb/db-before-t 2255 :edb/db-after-t 2256 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535568 50 #inst "2026-10-14T13:38:03.975Z" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186054799 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518175 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186054808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518189 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186059863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518174 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186059865 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518215 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186061885 1078 #edb/ref 17592186061888 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064064 1071 "Evaluate $\\log_{2} \\left( 2^{5} \\right).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064064 1071 "Evaluate $\\log_{2} \\left(2^{5} \\right)$." 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186064064 1074 "The logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The idea is that the log of a power of the base equals the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log_b(b^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=2$ and $x=5$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left(2^{5}\\right) = 5\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186064064 1074 "The logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The idea is that the log of a power of the base equals the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log_b(b^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=2$ and $x=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left( 2^{5}\\right) = 5.\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064065 1071 "Find the value of $\\log_{8}{8}$." 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186064065 1071 "Find the value of $\\log_{8}{8}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064065 1074 "We have the general rule that\n\n$$\n\\log_n n = 1\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=8$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{8}{8} =1\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186064065 1074 "We have the general rule that\n\n$$\n\\log_n n = 1.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{8}{8} =1.\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064066 1071 "Evaluate $\\log_{2} \\left(\\dfrac 1 {8} \\right)$." 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186064066 1071 "Evaluate $\\log_{2} \\left(\\dfrac 1 {8} \\right).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064066 1074 "First, we rewrite\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nas a power of $2$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8} = \\dfrac{1}{2^3} = 2^{-3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\log_{2} \\left(\\dfrac 1 {8} \\right)= \\log_{2} (2^{-3})\n$$\n\nNow we can proceed as usual. The log of a power of the base equals the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log_b(b^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=2$ and $x=-3$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}(2^{-3})= -3\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186064066 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ as a power of $2$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8} = \\dfrac{1}{2^3} = 2^{-3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\log_{2} \\left(\\dfrac 1 {8} \\right)= \\log_{2} (2^{-3}).\n$$\n\nNow we can proceed as usual. The log of a power of the base equals the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log_b(b^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=2$ and $x=-3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}( 2^{-3} )= -3.\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064067 1071 "Find the value of $\\log_{3}{9}$." 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186064067 1071 "Find the value of $\\log_{3}{9}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064067 1074 "First, we rewrite $9$ as a power of $3$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n9 = 3^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\log_{3}{9} = \\log_{3}(3^2)\n$$\n\nNow we can proceed as usual. The log of a power of the base equals the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log_b(b^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b = 3$ and $x = 2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{3}(3^2) = 2\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186064067 1074 "First, we rewrite $9$ as a power of $3$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n9 = 3^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\log_{3}{9} = \\log_{3}(3^2).\n$$\n\nNow we can proceed as usual. The log of a power of the base equals the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log_b(b^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b = 3$ and $x = 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{3}(3^2) = 2.\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518181 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518190 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518211 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064190 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518117 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064190 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518193 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064190 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518204 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064190 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518217 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064191 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518150 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064191 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518154 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064192 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518176 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064192 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518191 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064230 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518183 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518127 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064363 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518121 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064462 1050 420.0 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064638 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064638 1071 "${log}_{4}⁡1=$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186064638 1071 "What is $\\log_{4} 1$?" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186064638 1074 "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡1=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=4.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{4}⁡1=0\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186065355 1064 "We can evaluate some logarithm expressions by using the fact that the logarithm reverses the action of the exponent.\n\nThe idea is that the log of a power of the base equals the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log_b(b^x) = x\n$$\n\nFor example, in the case of\n\n$$\n\\log_2 \\left(2^{-1/2}\\right)\n$$\n\nwe have $b=2$ and\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_2\\left(2^{-1/2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186065355 1064 "We can evaluate some logarithm expressions by using the fact that the logarithm reverses the action of the exponent.\n\nThe idea is that the log of a power of the base equals the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log_b(b^x) = x\n$$\n\nFor example, in the case of $\\log_2 \\left( 2^{-1/2}\\right),$ we have $b=2$ and $x=-\\dfrac 1 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_2\\left(2^{-1/2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186065356 1064 "When the argument of the logarithm isn't written as an exponential, we can often still evaluate the logarithm. All we need to do is rewrite the argument so that it is in the form $b^x$, where $b$ is the base of the logarithm.\n\nFor instance, to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\log_2\\left(8\\right)\n$$\n\nwe rewrite $8$ as a power of $2$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n$8 = 2^{3}$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}{8} = \\log_{2}\\left(2^{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow we can proceed as before. The log of a power of the base equals the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log_b(b^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b = 2$ and $x = 3$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left(2^{3}\\right) = 3\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186065356 1064 "When the argument of the logarithm isn't written as an exponential, we can often still evaluate the logarithm. All we need to do is rewrite the argument so that it is in the form $b^x,$ where $b$ is the base of the logarithm.\n\nFor instance, to evaluate $\\log_2\\left( 8\\right),$ we rewrite $8$ as a power of $2$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n8 = 2^{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}{8} = \\log_{2}\\left(2^{3}\\right) .\n$$\n\nNow we can proceed as before. The log of a power of the base equals the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log_b(b^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b = 2$ and $x = 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left(2^{3}\\right) = 3.\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186065357 1064 "Recall that two special cases of exponentials are\n\n$$\nn^0 =1 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad n^1 =n\n$$\n\nWhat do these look like in logarithm form? We can use the definition of a logarithm to find out:\n\n> $a = {n}^{x}$ is equivalent to $\\log_{n}{a} = {x}$.\n\nUsing the above, we have that $n^0 =1$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\log_n 1 = 0\n$$\n\nAlso, we have that $n^1 =n$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\log_n n = 1\n$$\n\nThese are two special values for logarithms that are true for any base\n\n$$\nn>0, n \\neq 1\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** We should always assume that the base $n$ of the logarithm does not equal $1$. Recall that\n\n$$\n\\log_n{a} = x\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n$a = n^x$. If $n=1$ that transforms to\n$a = 1^x$.\nNow, we have the following two possibilities:\n\n- if $a \\neq 1$, we get that $a \\neq 1^x$ for any real $x$, and\n- if $a = 1$, we obtain that $1 = 1^x$ for all real $x$.\n\nIn both cases, the notation $\\log_1{a}$ does not make much sense." 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186065357 1064 "Recall that two special cases of exponentials are\n\n$$\nn^0 =1 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad n^1 =n.\n$$\n\nWhat do these look like in logarithm form? We can use the definition of a logarithm to find out:\n\n$a = {n}^{x}$ is equivalent to $\\log_{n}{a} = {x}.$\n\nUsing the above, we have that $n^0 =1$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\log_n 1 = 0.\n$$\n\nAlso, we have that $n^1 =n$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\log_n n = 1.\n$$\n\nThese are two special values for logarithms that are true for any base $n>0, n \\neq 1.$\n\n**Watch out!** We should always assume that the base $n$ of the logarithm does not equal $1.$ Recall that $\\log_n{a} = x$ is equivalent to\n$a = n^x.$ If $n=1$ that transforms to \n\n$$\na = 1^x.\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following two possibilities:\n\n- if $a \\neq 1,$ we get that $a \\neq 1^x$ for any real $x,$ and\n\n- if $a = 1,$ we obtain that $1 = 1^x$ for all real $x.$\n\nIn both cases, the notation $\\log_1{a}$ does not make much sense." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186067792 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067793 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186067792 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067794 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186067792 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067795 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186067792 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067796 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186067792 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067797 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186067792 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517525 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186067792 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517601 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186067792 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517655 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186067792 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517711 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186067792 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517835 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186067792 1078 #edb/ref 17592186517525 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068026 1075 #edb/ref 17592186068027 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186068026 1075 #edb/ref 17592186068028 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186068026 1075 #edb/ref 17592186068029 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186068026 1075 #edb/ref 17592186068031 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186068026 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517536 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068026 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517692 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068026 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517840 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068026 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517933 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068026 1078 #edb/ref 17592186517692 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068559 1071 "What are the solutions to $x^2 + 16 = 0$?" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186068559 1071 "What are the solutions to $x^2 + 16 = 0?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068559 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x^2 + 16 &= 0 \\\\[3pt] x^2 &= -16 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = \\pm \\sqrt{-16}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $x$ using the imaginary unit\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= ± \\sqrt{-16} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{16 \\cdot (-1)} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{16} \\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\\n&= ± 4\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = \\pm 4 \\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186068559 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2 + 16 &= 0 \\[3pt]\n\t\tx^2 &= -16\n}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = \\pm \\sqrt{-16}.\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $x$ using the imaginary unit $\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\, \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pm \\sqrt{-16} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{16\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x = \\pm 4 \\textrm{i}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068560 1071 "If $p^2 + 27 = 0$, then $p =$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186068560 1071 "If $p^2 + 27 = 0,$ then $p =$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068560 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} p^2 + 27 & = 0 \\\\[3pt] p^2 & = -27 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\np = \\pm \\sqrt{-27}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $p$ using the imaginary unit\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= ± \\sqrt{-27} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{27 \\cdot (-1)} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{27} \\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{3^{2} \\cdot 3} \\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\\n&= ± 3\\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\\n&= ± 3\\sqrt{3}\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\np = \\pm 3\\sqrt{3} \\, \\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186068560 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\np^2 + 27 & = 0\\[3pt]\n\np^2 & = -27 \n}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\np = \\pm \\sqrt{-27}.\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $p$ using the imaginary unit $\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\, \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =\\pm \\sqrt{-27} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{27\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{27}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}\\cdot 3}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $p = \\pm 3\\sqrt{3} \\, \\textrm{i}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068561 1071 "If $16z^2 + 49 = 0$, then $z =$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186068561 1071 "If $16z^2 + 49 = 0,$ then $z =$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068561 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 16z^2 + 49 & = 0 \\\\[5pt] 16z^2 & = -49 \\\\[5pt] z^2 & = -\\dfrac{49}{16} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\nz = \\pm \\sqrt{-\\dfrac{49}{16}}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $z$ using the imaginary unit\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= ± \\sqrt{-\\frac{49}{16}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16} \\cdot (-1)} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}} \\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{49}}{\\sqrt{16}} \\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{7}{4}\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nz=\\pm \\dfrac{7}{4} \\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186068561 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n16z^2 + 49 & = 0\\[5pt]\n\n16z^2 & = -49 \\[5pt]\n\nz^2 & = -\\dfrac{49}{16}\n}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\nz = \\pm \\sqrt{-\\dfrac{49}{16}}.\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $z$ using the imaginary unit $\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\, \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{49}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{49}}{\\sqrt{16}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{7}{4}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=\\pm \\dfrac{7}{4} \\textrm{i}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068562 1071 "What are the solutions of the equation $2y^2 + 9 = 0$?" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186068562 1071 "What are the solutions of the equation $2y^2 + 9 = 0?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068562 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2y^2 + 9 &= 0 \\\\[5pt] 2y^2 &= -9 \\\\[5pt] y^2 & = -\\dfrac{9}{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain\n\n$$\ny = \\pm \\sqrt{-\\dfrac{9}{2}}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $y$ using the imaginary unit\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= ± \\sqrt{-\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2} \\cdot (-1)} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= ± \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186068562 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2y^2 + 9 &= 0 \\[5pt]\n\n\t\t2y^2 &= -9\\[5pt]\n\ny^2 & = -\\dfrac{9}{2}\n}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain\n\n$$\ny = \\pm \\sqrt{-\\dfrac{9}{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $y$ using the imaginary unit $\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\, \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{i} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=\\pm \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\textrm{i}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068705 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518139 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068705 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518151 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518159 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518197 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518128 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518133 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518135 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186068991 1050 480.0 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186069437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186069437 1071 "What are the solutions of the equation $8y^{2} + 12 = 0$?" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186069437 1071 "What are the solutions of the equation $8{y}^{2}+12=0?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186069437 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{y}^{2}+12 & =0 \\\\ 8{y}^{2} & =-12 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{12}{8} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{3}{2}}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $y$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{i} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186069476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186069476 1071 "Find the solutions of the equation $25z^{2} + 36 = 0$." 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186069476 1071 "Find the solutions of the equation $25{z}^{2}+36=0.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186069476 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{z}^{2}+36 & =0 \\\\ 25{z}^{2} & =-36 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =-\\frac{36}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\nz=\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{36}{25}}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $z$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{36}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{36}{25}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{36}{25}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{36}}{\\sqrt{25}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{6}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186069964 1064 "When solving an equation, we sometimes require taking the square root of a negative number. In such cases, we will not end up with real solutions, but imaginary solutions.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's solve the equation\n\n$$\n2x^2 +8 = 0\n$$\n\nfor $x$. First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x^2 + 8 &= 0 \\\\[3pt] 2x^2 &= -8 \\\\[3pt] x^2 &= -4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = \\pm \\sqrt{-4}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $x$ using the imaginary unit\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= ± \\sqrt{-4} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{4 \\cdot (-1)} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{4} \\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\\n&= ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = \\pm 2 \\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186069964 1064 "When solving an equation, we sometimes require taking the square root of a negative number. In such cases, we will not end up with real solutions, but imaginary solutions.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's solve the equation $2x^2 +8 = 0$ for $x.$ First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2x^2 + 8 &= 0 \\[3pt]\n\t\t2x^2 &= -8 \\[3pt]\n\t\tx^2 &= -4\n}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = \\pm \\sqrt{-4}.\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $x$ using the imaginary unit $\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\, \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pm \\sqrt{-4} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{4\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x = \\pm 2 \\textrm{i}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186073738 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518125 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186073740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518116 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186073740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518188 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186073740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518216 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186073786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518169 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186073786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518172 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186073879 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518106 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186073880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518223 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186078406 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518111 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186078414 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518213 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186099169 1078 #edb/ref 17592186099171 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186099175 1078 #edb/ref 17592186099178 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186099831 1078 #edb/ref 17592186099833 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186100035 1078 #edb/ref 17592186100038 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186100640 1071 "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown below. Sketch a possible graph of $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/e3/e3feb58766bbbb4dc2ed0cd738c7a5d74d31a6d3bbc2e210010b9e5ecbc21da8.png)" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186100640 1071 "The graph of $y=f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below. Sketch a possible graph of $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/e3/e3feb58766bbbb4dc2ed0cd738c7a5d74d31a6d3bbc2e210010b9e5ecbc21da8.png)" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186100640 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points:\n\n$$\nx=-1\\quad \\text{and} \\quad x=1\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following:\n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n- If $f'(x) < 0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{not extr.}$ | $\\searrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical lines $x=-1$ and $x=1$. Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1)$, the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=-1$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27cfed2793c78d0abaa91c4d5480724303ebf729fa22befbd6142e8e54039e64.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and the right of $x=-1$. Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and goes down to the right of $x=1$. Furthermore, since $f'(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x = 1$, we must have $f''(x) > 0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x = 1$, we must have $f''(x) < 0$. So, $x =1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\n![](images/15/15842026cf3d019dcfd31c0f4f9aad54a4c8465dc1464feaa2d07b45e109ef74.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n![](images/0f/0f7e45db7621e8ecd7d915d49d14a8656070365023f12d7239c435f7da8f72fc.png)" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186100640 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$$\nx=-1\\quad \\text{and} \\quad x=1\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $f'(x) <         0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{not extr.}$ | $\\searrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical lines $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=-1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27cfed2793c78d0abaa91c4d5480724303ebf729fa22befbd6142e8e54039e64.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and goes down to the right of $x=1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since $f'(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x = 1$, we must have $f''(x) >       0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x = 1$, we must have $f''(x) <    0.$ So, $x =1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\n![](images/15/15842026cf3d019dcfd31c0f4f9aad54a4c8465dc1464feaa2d07b45e109ef74.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/0f/0f7e45db7621e8ecd7d915d49d14a8656070365023f12d7239c435f7da8f72fc.png)" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186100641 1071 "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given below. Sketch a possible graph of $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/23/23c5620bdd59a6359eaa9bad6580b4b844237bead64b7138f7472b65c7e17c1b.png)" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186100641 1071 "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given below. Sketch a possible graph of $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/23/23c5620bdd59a6359eaa9bad6580b4b844237bead64b7138f7472b65c7e17c1b.png)" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186100641 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points:\n\n$$\nx=1\\quad \\text{and} \\quad x=2\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following:\n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n- If $f'(x) < 0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2, \\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\ne