$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is constant to the left of $x=0$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ represents a section of a straight line on $(-\\infty ,0).$ Also, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the right of $x=0$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/d8/d8cf7b97d4e9356b275b669d59b0498286bc673d55fbc533891daac369d5cd35.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/5f/5fc6864d1b7530ecd4fa1b151390b85d32a5d0628d0f51160cfaab7fc61e33b9.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186101445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186101445 1071 "If $f^{′}(x) =-\\mid x + 2 \\mid$, which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $y = f(x)$?" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186101445 1071 "If ${f}^{′}(x)=-|x+2|,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186101445 1074 "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/0b/0bfc917724b8339ef52888f287f2bc1c69fca41e9207f42ff3d83a633fe3cc0a.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-2$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d032eca4d1a29d5a7809dfd5c43cce52b8a0b66f1c8bdc9b9747cf704da524f5.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and is decreasing $↘$ on $(-2,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left of $x=-2$ and goes down to the right of $x=-2.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=-2$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x=-2$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, $x=-2$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/ba/ba965ae91587d92beee6b5acb9af809167bb1de01fe1c2cdb8b0637244356e7f.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/fb/fbe2e07c3c82e1140e2e9d687020ece8dad208251dfeec2bddbd27bc5c44841d.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186101479 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186101479 1071 "![](images/58/58ebecdd8284dff6e807465f29c890bd87ec763896242cbff2bd1d18a4728f49.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y = f(x)$, is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y = f(x)$?" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186101479 1071 "![](images/58/58ebecdd8284dff6e807465f29c890bd87ec763896242cbff2bd1d18a4728f49.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186101479 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-2\\,$ and $\\,x=3$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=-2$ and $x=3.$\n\n- We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-2.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-2,3),$ the point at $x=3$ must be higher than the point at $x=-2.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/ac/ac3ad854bdef4e5c7011a81a14da722cef44657e8024a39533179513589d0867.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-2,3)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-2.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-2,3)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(3,\\infty )$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=3.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=1$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x=1$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, $x=1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\n![](images/81/81870d6d4fbba512d353788dd06062e380baeb672002f150b2c54eeac73a1bd7.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/b2/b243c1cfb9da30100aee5f215e02c5eb11769cef1ba4b2b5b74f2879e8a34e10.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186102005 1064 "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Let's use this to sketch a possible graph of the original function, $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/6c/6cd6c0fa999d571b54b7408856ecc8d091c2f4bb16b9f7bf85eb7f91b05629e4.png)\n\nRecall that the critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points:\n\n$$\nx=-3\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad x=1\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following:\n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n- If $f'(x) < 0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical lines $x=-3$ and $x=1$. Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-3,1)$, the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=-3$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb0de4712344b43d32343f4d9fbc11d3dbc5c96df858f27bee599a3ffdd448ce.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-3)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-3,1)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=-3$.Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-3,1)$ and increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(1,\\infty)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=1$.\n\n![](images/34/3498651b6a642f59068f23e8ed9ddd211bfb76ab4d0bf8e28cefd84d1a4b2aed.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n![](images/25/257203aa25e423bf82ce745a6a8a3563aa80e414e9dfadf0be64629624cbce0e.png)" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186102005 1064 "The graph of $y=f'(x),$ the derivative of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Let's use this to sketch a possible graph of the original function, $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/6c/6cd6c0fa999d571b54b7408856ecc8d091c2f4bb16b9f7bf85eb7f91b05629e4.png)\n\nRecall that the critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$$\nx=-3\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad x=1\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $f'(x) <         0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical lines $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-3,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=-3,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb0de4712344b43d32343f4d9fbc11d3dbc5c96df858f27bee599a3ffdd448ce.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-3)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-3,1)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=-3.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-3,1)$ and increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(1,\\infty)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=1.$\n\n![](images/34/3498651b6a642f59068f23e8ed9ddd211bfb76ab4d0bf8e28cefd84d1a4b2aed.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/25/257203aa25e423bf82ce745a6a8a3563aa80e414e9dfadf0be64629624cbce0e.png)" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186102006 1064 "Let's draw our possible function $y=f(x)$ once more. The graph of its derivative $y=f'(x)$ is also shown.\n\n![](images/18/189c181d3767960f39fc0cfcb06dac5bf6fca07e303b11999307551d081ffc1e.png)\n\nIn reality, there are infinitely many possible graphs of $f(x)$ that can be drawn if we are given only the graph of $f'(x)$. We can always shift the graph up and down as we please, because the slopes of the tangent lines do not change. For the problem above, any of the following graphs would be valid:\n\n![](images/e4/e49f65d7306192d73001552084f06cf39731f38f3ccc14dd6b2f5f7641ec43aa.png)\n\nIn this topic, we will focus on sketching a *possible* graph of $y=f(x)$ given the graph of $y=f'(x)$. But bear in mind that whatever $y=f(x)$ we sketch, we can get an equally valid answer by shifting or $y=f(x)$ curve up or down by an arbitrary amount.\n\nThe only exception is if we're given a point on the curve $y=f(x)$. If we're given a point, this has the effect of *fixing* our answer to a particular curve. We'll see an example of this next." 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186102006 1064 "Let's draw our possible function $y=f(x)$ once more. The graph of its derivative $y=f'(x)$ is also shown.\n\n![](images/18/189c181d3767960f39fc0cfcb06dac5bf6fca07e303b11999307551d081ffc1e.png)\n\nIn reality, there are infinitely many possible graphs of $f(x)$ that can be drawn if we are given only the graph of $f'(x).$ We can always shift the graph up and down as we please, because the slopes of the tangent lines do not change. For the problem above, any of the following graphs would be valid:\n\n![](images/e4/e49f65d7306192d73001552084f06cf39731f38f3ccc14dd6b2f5f7641ec43aa.png)\n\nIn this topic, we will focus on sketching a *possible* graph of $y=f(x)$ given the graph of $y=f'(x).$ But bear in mind that whatever $y=f(x)$ we sketch, we can get an equally valid answer by shifting or $y=f(x)$ curve up or down by an arbitrary amount. \n\nThe only exception is if we're given a point on the curve $y=f(x).$ If we're given a point, this has the effect of *fixing* our answer to a particular curve. We'll see an example of this next." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186105750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518119 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186105751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518185 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186105751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518209 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186105752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518110 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186105752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518205 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186119756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518114 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186119756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518160 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186119861 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518101 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186119938 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518149 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186119938 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518167 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186119942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518112 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186119942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518196 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186124451 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518199 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186124458 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518124 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186124459 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518155 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186124459 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518218 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186124610 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518118 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186124610 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518157 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186126206 1078 #edb/ref 17592186126207 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186129235 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518120 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186129235 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518210 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186129451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186129451 1071 "Given that $a$ is an **irrational** number, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $17$ is rational\n\nII. $17a$ is rational\n\nIII. $a\\cdot 17$ is irrational" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186129451 1071 "Given that $a$ is an irrational number, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $17$ is rational\n2. $17a$ is rational\n3. $a \\cdot 17$ is irrational" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186129451 1074 "Recall that: \n\n- the product of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the product of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. The product of a rational number ($17$) and an irrational number ($a$) is irrational.\n\n- Statement III is true. We know from part II that $17a$ is irrational. And since multiplication is commutative, we conclude that $a\\cdot 17$ is irrational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186133452 1078 #edb/ref 17592186133456 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186134569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186134569 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{3},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{1},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186134569 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n$f_{1}(t) = t^{2}, f_{2}(t) = t^{3}, f_{3}(t) = t$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[- 1, 1]$ of continuous functions over $[- 1, 1]$ is defined as\n$⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = \\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)dt$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f_{1}(t)⊥f_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $f_{2}(t)⊥f_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S = {f_{1}(t), f_{2}(t), f_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨f_{1}, f_{3}⟩ = 0$.*" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186134569 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{6}}{6}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{6}}{6}-\\frac{(-1)^{6}}{6} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{3},t⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\cdot t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{(-1)^{5}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186138414 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518113 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186138415 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518108 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186138416 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518200 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186147544 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518126 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186152126 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518173 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186154797 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517768 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186154797 1078 #edb/ref 17592186517768 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186154798 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517828 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186154798 1078 #edb/ref 17592186517828 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186154800 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517814 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186154800 1078 #edb/ref 17592186517814 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186157040 1071 "Suppose $\\mathbf a=[6,\\, -1,\\,2]^T$ and $\\mathbf b=[4,\\, -4,\\,0]^T$. Find the Manhattan distance $d_1$ between $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$." 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186157040 1071 "Suppose $\\mathbf a=[6,\\, -1,\\,2]^T$ and $\\mathbf b=[4,\\, -4,\\,0]^T.$ Find the Manhattan distance $d_1$ between $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186157040 1074 "Recall that the Manhattan distance is defined as\n\n$$\nd_1(\\mathbf a,\\mathbf b) =\\|\\mathbf a-\\left.\\mathbf b\\\\right|_1\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $\\mathbf v$,\n\n$$\n\\\\begin{vmatrix}\