\n&\\approx 2.060\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186157041 1074 "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\nd_p(\\mathbf a,\\mathbf b) =\\|\\mathbf a-\\mathbf b\\|_p\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $\\mathbf v,$\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|_p = \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^N |v_i|^p \\right)^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{4}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{4} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]‖}_{4} \\\\  & ={(|6-7{|}^{4}+|-3-(-1){|}^{4}+|2-1{|}^{4})}^{1/4} \\\\  & ={18}^{1/4} \\\\  & ≈2.060,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186157042 1071 "Suppose $\\mathbf a=[-2,\\, 6,\\, -7]^T$ and $\\mathbf b=[8,\\, -9,\\, -9]^T$. Find the Chebyshev distance $d_\\infty$ between $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$." 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186157042 1071 "Suppose $\\mathbf a=[-2,\\, 6,\\, -7]^T$ and $\\mathbf b=[8,\\, -9,\\, -9]^T.$ Find the Chebyshev distance $d_\\infty$ between $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186157042 1074 "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\nd_\\infty(\\mathbf a,\\mathbf b) =\\|\\mathbf a-\\left.\\mathbf b\\\\right|_\\infty\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $\\mathbf v$,\n\n$$\n\\left.\\|\\mathbf v\\\\right|_\\infty = \\operatorname{max}(\\begin{vmatrix}v_1 &, & v_2 &,\\ldots, & v_N\\end{vmatrix})\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd_{∞}(a, b) &= ‖a - b‖_{∞} \\\\\n&= ‖\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}‖_{∞} \\\\\n&= \\max(\\begin{vmatrix}-2 - 8 &, & 6 - (-9) &, & -7 - (-9)\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\max(10, 15, 2) \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186157042 1074 "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\nd_\\infty(\\mathbf