 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -60 \\\\ -60 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186162356 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -6\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, W = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W$, and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $WAx = Wb$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & -60 \\\\ -60 & b\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nwhat is the value of $a + b + c$?" 13194139535568 17592186045417 false] [17592186162356 1074 "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\