5417 true] [17592186518097 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048716 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518097 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048792 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518097 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049061 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518097 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049506 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518097 1089 #edb/ref 17592186518098 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518097 1090 #uuid "a61c5482-22b6-538d-8ab8-e8888d96d94e" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518098 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518246 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518098 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518254 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518098 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518258 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518098 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518278 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518098 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518284 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518098 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518296 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518098 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518305 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518098 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518306 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518098 1085 "![](images/75/75f62c88d82d57bb43d69db5b62afd0f990b0a148c5d22cb48b2cfefe7f331aa.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,6].$ It's known that $f(-3)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ consists of two line segments and a semicircle, as shown above." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518098 1086 #uuid "33ad15c6-26fa-53bb-a622-360a616b1dba" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518098 1087 #edb/ref 17592186518278 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518058 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518099 1069 "q-20507" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518099 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518099 1071 "![](images/2d/2dd50d4970f5c738c7cc350b8aee4668a6ec9ce4b1444698c5ecd737d01f82ea.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518099 1072 #uuid "01274ce5-42e5-5d91-b593-1351d7ffb44f" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518099 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-2$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9eae519cd7ca99aa85fe62c9fa8276a7455cbc869587296405c6a6b3caf2ee30.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-2,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and the right of $x=-2.$\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/1a/1a2fd6311c5845885bfd7ed9118091a5c81c4fea8c81587db79b203a51b9e1de.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/f6/f6e473044a84d215de98e3739bea83ccaf107231ccb273c8e45db91221f05edb.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518065 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518100 1069 "q-277239" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518100 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518100 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt[9]{-8-3x}+2.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518100 1072 #uuid "01c856f3-ee69-579a-9f89-fe0fbee2a037" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518100 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$ we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[9]{-8-3x}+2=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[9]{-8-3x}+2 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[9]{-8-3x} & =-2 \\\\ (\\sqrt[9]{-8-3x})^{9} & =(-2)^{9} \\\\ -8-3x & =-512 \\\\ -3x & =-504 \\\\ x & =168\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=168.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518000 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518101 1069 "q-5658" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518101 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518101 1071 "![](images/2e/2e054a16d93658d53cc9dc16e3fe5544b9a007721d1d9c2f87581799a6257797.png)\n\nWhat is the diameter of a circle that has an area of $36\\pi ?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518101 1072 #uuid "0207e47c-2942-5ced-8414-95264c8bde68" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518101 1074 "The formula for the area of a circle is $A=\\pi {r}^{2}$, where $r$ is the radius. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 36\\pi  & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 36\\cancel{\\pi } & =\\cancel{\\pi }{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diameter is\n\n$$\nd=2\\cdot r=2\\cdot 6=12.\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517997 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518102 1069 "q-81019" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518102 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518102 1071 "![](images/2a/2ab398982e30d4b0ce633b77541596f3f5848e1b0a25539037ec3ee0fc557d4d.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518102 1072 #uuid "02de7b14-03bb-5d7e-b753-b48cb91786a1" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518102 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:  \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1.$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\n \n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical line $x=-1$ and choose an arbitrary point on it.\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/77/7719107149a10c6e5a22e6c343b532f3580582ebd78d3667aad5f2684d2f219d.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-1,0)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=0$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0).$ Also, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is constant and positive  to the right of $x=0$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ represents a section of an increasing straight line on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/d8/d81008f3d06f41f53a389fa2f36a7b12845fa3dae5a2707a739bbb500c8b4e56.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/76/763163652b41a7b3cc0b7a6b3faca4799cc073238a50afd9bd3d9c7767c32793.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518034 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518103 1069 "q-131253" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518103 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518103 1071 "Given that the point $(2,\\sqrt{5})$ lies on the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ find ${f}^{″}(2).$\n\n${f}^{″}(2)\\,=\\,$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518103 1072 #uuid "0343bcd3-e974-592d-9f5b-2241e197ca26" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518103 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({f}^{′}(x))\n$$\n\nis the slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$ \n\nNotice that the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ on $x\\in [-3,3]$ is a semicircle of a radius $3$ centered at $(0,0).$ Its equation (for $y>0$) is given by\n\n$$\n(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}={3}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(2,\\,\\sqrt{5})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518033 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518104 1069 "q-115653" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518104 1071 "![](images/28/28a16cda2f97557c23908fcd45928da5d4e11102343c33eaf07d1990fb0b6baf.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518104 1072 #uuid "06d5e4de-57e7-5985-961d-b5e59bdab913" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518104 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=0$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=0,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea6ac357860556acba25d5510ff0e65a4b66acc65e035cd218715b0e96807519.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-\\infty ,0)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(0,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=0.$\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/2a/2aafc298a8a7b0856d339582bb1236e9ae97c728eba103c703998e14455fdde5.png)\n\n \n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/04/04ec8de5abfecc23f3b4fb844b4e725e2a4f94aeb12bef61cda6356a6522826c.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518032 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518105 1069 "q-288455" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518105 1071 "The equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+ux+v={x}^{2}-x+1\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $u$ and $v$ are constants. What is the value of $u+v?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518105 1072 #uuid "090c9908-d9d1-5e59-8b6d-0af06c1b251a" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518105 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $u,$ and on the right-hand side it is $-1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nu=-1.\n$$\n\n- The constant term on the left-hand side is $v,$ and on the right-hand side it is $1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nv=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518014 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518106 1069 "q-60241" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518106 1071 "What is missing in the following sentence?\n\nTo make a delicious soup, Alice requires $3$ grams of ginger powder and $4$ grams of garlic powder, among other ingredients, so she needs $\\boxed{}$ grams of ginger powder for each gram of garlic." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518106 1072 #uuid "0b38d5e8-3d9c-538c-a4ad-3e7a464487ac" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518106 1074 "The ratio of the number of grams of ginger powder to the number of grams of garlic powder is  $3:4.$ \n\nWe can rewrite this ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nThis means that there is $\\frac{3}{4}$ grams of ginger powder for each gram of garlic powder." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517974 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518107 1069 "q-49040" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518107 1071 "If ${f}^{′}(x)=|x+5|,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518107 1072 #uuid "0bf75373-8a0d-58e6-9585-31096e6c716e" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518107 1074 "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/ae/ae96c488c78fb207567c6ae0ea7497782af1e840a7852bd79b57902d45972a01.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-5$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $-5$ | $(-5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-5,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/2c/2c67c136914eba7687d96a66e737e2f1f7d96144d4535b25f0ac48e16aed8396.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-\\infty ,-5)$ and on $(-5,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left of $x=-5$ and goes up to the right of $x=-5.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing to the left of $x=-5$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0$, and, since it is increasing to the right of $x=-5$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, $x=-5$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave down to concave up.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/97/971e495bbe1d7c502a1e65ec2b0764f0057158e3fe989261245382223020e872.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/00/00320e4c614d3ba3051ffca74a005113b057668488c12d50c5c497d613ce7492.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518091 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518108 1069 "q-115488" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518108 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518108 1071 "Consider the table below. Which of the following is a true statement about the relationship between $x$ and $y?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0.5$ | $20$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ |\n| $4$ | $2.5$ |" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518108 1072 #uuid "11ab4bcc-ff94-5b73-9790-5edd61c08193" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518108 1074 "If $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ then we must have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nfor some constant $k.$ Note that we can rewrite the above equation as follows.\n\n$$\nx\\cdot y=k\n$$\n\nSo, we will compute $x\\cdot y=k$ for each row in the table. If we get the same value of $k$ for each row, then we can conclude that $y$ is inversely proportional to $x.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $x\\cdot y=k$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0.5$ | $20$ | $0.5\\cdot 20=10$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $2\\cdot 5=10$ |\n| $4$ | $2.5$ | $4\\cdot 2.5=10$ |\n\nThe results are all the same: in each row, we have $k=10.$ Therefore, $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ and the constant of variation is $k=10.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518026 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518109 1069 "q-115938" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518109 1071 "![](images/02/02bec11dec3fc03b97b918c392969b246e6b2751d78c0b32b796bdeb4e6eaaca.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518109 1072 #uuid "140bcd57-110f-51e2-8837-ad085a0b4faa" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518109 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:  \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1$  \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical line $x=-1$ and choose an arbitrary point on it.\n\n$\\,$ So, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/90/9062dcd04f6ca0dcb21453596ebd007ccf91a33f5092a601d24e401e888a20f1.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-1,\\infty )$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Moreover, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=2$ and to the right of $x=2$, although with different slopes, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,2)$  and on $(2,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/fb/fbd0da8fbd98e0b83cd1efb82d5e1f1aca1bf40108e11c6a401da46e514a133a.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/95/95cc3ce5bab09a3e3ea32571e2156d13605b85bf8f026c7c35b5dd51d045c7fd.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518057 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518110 1069 "q-223201" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518110 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518110 1071 "![](images/33/33c1016cb2353e5332a5e04e8f377d4fa7d76e1b569a3532a45c5a068cc6d255.png)\n\nFor the quadrilateral above, determine the measure of the exterior angle of $∠C.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518110 1072 #uuid "143c348d-4bc6-5a45-bf95-01d78dce959f" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518110 1074 "Since the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6x+{23}^{∘})+(17x-{4}^{∘})+(21x+{8}^{∘})+(4x-{3}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 48x+{24}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 48x & ={336}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={7}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of the exterior angle of $∠C$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21x+{8}^{∘} & =21({7}^{∘})+{8}^{∘} \\\\  & ={147}^{∘}+8 \\\\  & ={155}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517995 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518024 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518111 1069 "q-131249" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518111 1071 "Find all the values of $x$ in the interval $(-6,6)$ where the function $f(x)$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$f(x)$ is strictly increasing for $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,,\\,$  \n{{field-2}}\n$)$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518111 1072 #uuid "168e3b6f-08dd-5743-a200-729abea32e10" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518111 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n* If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on $(a,b).$\n\n* If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on $(a,b).$\n\nThe graph below shows that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-3,3).$\n\n![](images/c7/c72ad85a6998493f29289074a4750f2830e8fdefe51d6f81536257abfb8ae008.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly increasing when $x\\in (-3,3).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518070 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518112 1069 "q-101158" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518112 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{{t}^{2}+t-6}{{t}^{2}-9t+14}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518112 1072 #uuid "17d3a51b-87c6-5439-b5d7-1ae7b5260460" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518112 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{t}^{2}+t-6}{{t}^{2}-9t+14} & =\\frac{{2}^{2}+2-6}{{2}^{2}-9(2)+14}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{t}^{2}+t-6}{{t}^{2}-9t+14} & =\\frac{(t+3)(t-2)}{(t-2)(t-7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{t+3}{t-7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{{t}^{2}+t-6}{{t}^{2}-9t+14} & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{t+3}{t-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+3}{2-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(-5)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518075 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518113 1069 "q-217248" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518113 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518113 1071 "Suppose that $y$ varies inversely with $x$ and that $y=\\frac{3}{4}$ when $x=12.$ Then, if $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518113 1072 #uuid "1c37fb77-7c99-58f3-b32e-45223dbee74b" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518113 1074 "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=\\frac{3}{4}$ when $x=12.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{k}{12} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 12 & =k \\\\ 9 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{9}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{9}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =9÷(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\frac{3}{1}) \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517980 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518114 1069 "q-53582" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518114 1071 "What is the area of a circular dinner plate given that its radius is $8\\,\\text{cm}?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518114 1072 #uuid "1c7ac009-3403-59c4-adad-c97136633e2b" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518114 1074 "![](images/72/7223cab003af57279fc16d8044bd9eae6dbfa6bbfe41433b851ebdafd52e91da.png)\n\n \n\nThe area of the dinner plate  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {8}^{2} \\\\  & =64\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518115 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518048 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518115 1069 "q-20537" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518115 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518115 1071 "![](images/56/562596b4267c6a13ef13c4f20e7861b00675e85febc43653efd141d720900804.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(0)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518115 1072 #uuid "1fbb657d-32e1-57fb-9710-afc011b6ab8e" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518115 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1\\,$ and $\\,x=0.$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{not extr.}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=-1,$ and $x=0.$\n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-1,0)$, the point at $x=0$ must be lower than the point at $x=-1.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/50/504a1af2c6d97200a3b211e8a7c67298082cd3cffbf866851afa63f7162dcce3.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and on $(-1,0)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and down to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-1,0)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(0,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=0.$\n\n![](images/6e/6e6c1b9806b3dbf340255884722429a67dc3a7cd55d8cc0a0bbeeb48dcae1c87.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/10/10185719d14812cd4f58a592f106fe4fd81540ab6f6177f61d77bd6ec4e5d6c4.png)" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518116 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517983 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518116 1069 "q-59905" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518116 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518116 1071 "There are $8$ students with blonde hair and $12$ students with black hair in a ballet class. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $2:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $2$ students with blonde hair for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ students with black hair." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518116 1072 #uuid "210d98b1-d520-5974-b07e-4d5e5c06b223" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518116 1074 "Since there are $8$ students with blonde hair and $12$ students with black hair, the ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $8:12.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,8 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,12 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $2:3$ is equivalent to $8:12.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $2:3\\,$ because there are $2$ students with blonde hair for every $3\\,$ students with black hair.\"\n\nHence, the missing number is $3.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518117 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518050 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518117 1069 "q-99676" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518117 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518117 1071 "${log}_{13}⁡1=$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518117 1072 #uuid "25969868-abb0-5d93-a9b3-c99a6eb6f487" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518117 1074 "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡1=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=13.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{13}⁡1=0\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518118 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518074 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518118 1069 "q-129665" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518118 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518118 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=2x,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=-2z,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)=-2y.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,-1.02,-1.03).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518118 1072 #uuid "2a3146e0-f24e-5488-9d01-55dfe24a4858" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518118 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,-1)+∇f(1,-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1),z-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{z}(1,-1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,-1,-1),$ ${f}_{y}(1,-1,-1)$ and ${f}_{z}(1,-1,-1),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1) & =2(1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,-1,-1) & =-2(-1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{z}(1,-1,-1) & =-2(-1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-1+⟨2,2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.01,-1.02,-1.03)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.01,y=-1.02$ and $z=-1.03,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,-1.02,-1.03) & ≈-1+⟨2,2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,-1.02+1,-1.03+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨2,2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,-0.02,-0.03⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+2(0.01)+2(-0.02)+2(-0.03) \\\\  & ≈-1+0.02-0.04-0.06 \\\\  & ≈-1.08\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518018 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518119 1069 "q-223356" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518119 1071 "![](images/9e/9e40dfd3842ec7a7b5de16d7938988e86209960ae9cba510bbac004b0bb73d94.png)\n\nFor the pentagon above, the measure of the exterior angle of $∠B$ is{{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518119 1072 #uuid "2ac19296-c890-536b-9e9a-47b0cd74c840" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518119 1074 "Let us call $x$ the exterior angle of $∠B.$ Since the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{58}^{∘}+x+{65}^{∘}+{63}^{∘}+{97}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ x+{283}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={77}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518120 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518059 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518120 1069 "q-133670" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518120 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518120 1071 "Given that $a$ is an **irrational** number, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{7}$ is irrational\n\nII. $\\frac{1}{7}+a$ is always irrational\n\nIII. $a-\\frac{1}{7}$ is sometimes rational and sometimes irrational" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518120 1072 #uuid "2b569a4a-634a-5c5d-8e62-c4297125cda8" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518120 1074 "Recall that: \n\n- the sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\frac{1}{7}$ is rational and $a$ is irrational, their sum is always an irrational number.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\frac{1}{7}$ is rational and $a$ is irrational, their difference is always an irrational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518121 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518054 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518121 1069 "q-2129" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518121 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518121 1071 "$5\\cdot (-5)=$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518121 1072 #uuid "2b95da98-1f5f-5fa5-ad72-3959a1d46504" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518121 1074 "We have a product of a positive number $(5)$ and a negative number $(-5).$ So the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot (-5)=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply $5$ by $5$ without any signs: $5\\cdot 5=25.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n5\\cdot (-5)=-25.\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518122 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518046 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518122 1069 "q-254902" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518122 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518122 1071 "Suppose $a=[5,\\,-3,\\,4]^{T},$ and $b=[4,\\,-1,\\,2]^{T},$ and $p=4.$ Find the Minkowski distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\nRounded to three decimal places, ${d}_{p}(a,b)≈$ \n{{field-1}}." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518122 1072 #uuid "2ca9d6e1-42a8-5184-8cd9-770775f3f1b2" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518122 1074 "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{p}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{p}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{p}={({\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{p})}^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{4}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{4} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]‖}_{4} \\\\  & ={(|5-4{|}^{4}+|-3-(-1){|}^{4}+|4-2{|}^{4})}^{1/4} \\\\  & ={33}^{1/4} \\\\  & ≈2.397.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518056 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518123 1069 "q-131248" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518123 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518123 1071 "$f(6)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518123 1072 #uuid "35ae1f33-1dce-5ef7-af9a-1cf242b3dd81" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518123 1074 "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-f(-3) \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) \\\\ f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ on $[-3,6]$ consists of two parts: \n\n* an arc of a quarter circle for $x\\in [-3,3],$ which lies above the $x$-axis, and \n\n* a line segment for $x\\in [3,6],$ which lies below the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,3].$ Earlier, we found that\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,6].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ab \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the original integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find $f(6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6) \\\\  & =2\\pi -6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518094 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518124 1069 "q-86533" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518124 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518124 1071 "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,-1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(2,0).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518124 1072 #uuid "36bc1d86-9d95-5fad-8592-9df3687ca62a" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518124 1074 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,0⟩-⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨3,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8x(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}),\\,36{y}^{3}-24{x}^{2}y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1) & =⟨8(-1)\\cdot (2(-1)^{2}-3(-1)^{2}),36(-1)^{3}-24(-1)^{2}(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,-1) & =∇f(-1,-1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518084 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518125 1069 "q-236322" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518125 1071 "The ratio $3:2$ is equivalent to the ratio{{field-1}}$:10.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518125 1072 #uuid "3851cb9c-32ab-50a5-b26d-1f3dac949677" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518125 1074 "To find the ratio equivalent to $3:2$, we multiply both numbers by the same value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,\\,5 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓\\times \\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\boxed{} &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,10 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get $10$ from $2,$ we multiply $2$ by $5.$ So, to get $\\,\\boxed{}\\,$ from $3,$ we multiply $3$ by $5$ as well:\n\n$$\n3\\times 5=15\n$$\n\nThe ratio $3:2$ is equivalent to $15:10.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517987 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518126 1069 "q-350272" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518126 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518126 1071 "Ella and Nora run bakeries. In the previous week, $10%$ of Ella’s pastries were gluten-free, while the decimal proportion of Nora’s pastries that were gluten-free was $0.05.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Less than one fourth of Ella's pastries were gluten-free.\n\nII. More than one fourth of Nora's pastries were gluten-free.\n\nIII. Ella had a greater percentage of gluten-free pastries compared to Nora." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518126 1072 #uuid "38eccaa1-2de4-5013-b379-9718f328c233" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518126 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n10%<\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 100%=25%,\n$$\n\nless than one-fourth of Ella's pastries were gluten-free.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n0.05<\\frac{1}{4}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, less than one-fourth of Nora's pastries were gluten-free.\n\n- Statement III is true. We can compare $10%$ and $0.05$ by converting the decimal into a percent.\n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.05\\times 100=5%\n$$\n\nSince $10%>5%,$ Ella had a **greater** percentage of gluten-free pastries compared to Nora.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518010 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518127 1069 "q-236832" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518127 1071 "$6-43=${{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518127 1072 #uuid "38fc7adf-0452-58e7-9840-3560c89df130" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518127 1074 "Since $43$ is larger than $6,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem:\n\n$$\n43-6\n$$\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,3\\end{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,13\\end{aligned} \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  & \\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n43-6=37.\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n6-43=-37.\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518035 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518128 1069 "q-112392" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518128 1071 "Solve the equation $64{x}^{2}+25=0.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518128 1072 #uuid "400405cc-0be2-5aff-a15c-d39026e0c0e5" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518128 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64{x}^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ 64{x}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{25}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{25}{64}}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $x$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{25}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{64}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{64}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{25}}{\\sqrt{64}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{8}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517981 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518129 1069 "q-158687" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518129 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 7 \\\\ 7 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518129 1072 #uuid "4429ddaa-a525-58c0-92b3-0e53fe11c811" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518129 1074 "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {3}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 9 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}14 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 17\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=14+17+(-11)=20.\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518042 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518130 1069 "q-93093" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518130 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt[4]{7x+11}+3.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518130 1072 #uuid "442d41d4-64b6-5c13-9965-e1b753377abb" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518130 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{7x+11}+3=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{7x+11}+3 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{7x+11} & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any solutions since its left-hand side is always nonnegative, while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no root." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518090 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518131 1069 "q-115622" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518131 1071 "![](images/94/944d7dfa7895e5eecc2d11065e268fceba725c8181ffa8283d1fcac39d2d5a98.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(0)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518131 1072 #uuid "451559e6-da00-5040-a946-bd30072c74e4" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518131 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=1\\,$ and $\\,x=3.5$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3.5)$ | $3.5$ | $(3.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=3.5.$ \n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=0.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(1,3.5),$ the point at $x=3.5$ must be higher than the point at $x=1.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/4e/4e6fcbb39c9750bb41a2b505325a1b11a633ca749d740aea7c6737bee7db7f7a.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,1)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(1,3.5)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(1,3.5)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(3.5,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=3.5.$\n\n![](images/c0/c010f5bb4dcee1241211beefa90e9496045f2b5e5ddc861bd703e8d0cf0f3276.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/49/493dd437fa00fe3d3056dcb6c2a0b291db27d8bdf96e96ee5366d696bf9433e2.png)" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518047 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518132 1069 "q-102549" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518132 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=5\\sqrt[4]{4x+17}-15.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518132 1072 #uuid "4df596f3-7605-5476-aed1-e33a4f9ad66d" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518132 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n5\\sqrt[4]{4x+17}-15=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt[4]{4x+17}-15 & =0 \\\\ 5\\sqrt[4]{4x+17} & =15 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{4x+17} & =3 \\\\ (\\sqrt[4]{4x+17})^{4} & ={3}^{4} \\\\ 4x+17 & =81 \\\\ 4x & =64 \\\\ x & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt[4]{4\\cdot 16+17}-15 & =0 \\\\ 5\\sqrt[4]{81}-15 & =0 \\\\ 5\\cdot 3-15 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=16.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518082 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518133 1069 "q-113227" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518133 1071 "What are the solutions of the equation $27{y}^{2}+63=0?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518133 1072 #uuid "4f3f1aa3-5d05-5a94-a6c1-299187fd64c9" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518133 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27{y}^{2}+63 & =0 \\\\ 27{y}^{2} & =-63 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{63}{27} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{7}{3}}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $y$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{7}{3}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{i} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517989 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518134 1069 "q-131247" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518134 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518134 1071 "Find the value of $f(3).$\n\n$f(3)\\,=\\,$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518134 1072 #uuid "4f9b4143-02aa-5852-84a2-99409167bad1" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518134 1074 "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(3)-f(-3) \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(3)-(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) \\\\ f(3) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe definite integral of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ over $[-3,3]$ is the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis. The corresponding region is a semicircle, so we find the area using the formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+\\frac{9\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518083 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518135 1069 "q-131252" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518135 1071 "${f}^{″}(4)=${{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518135 1072 #uuid "5064a9ef-4f8e-5fbe-8d7d-2164ebe22645" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518135 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=({f}^{′}(x))^{′}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that $4\\in [3,6],$ where the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is represented by a straight line segment. So, ${f}^{″}(4)$ is just the slope of this segment.\n\nSince this segment passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,0)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(6,-4),$ we can compute its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{-4-0}{6-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{″}(4)=-\\frac{4}{3}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518088 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518089 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518136 1069 "q-131254" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518136 1071 "How many inflection points does the function $f(x)$ have inside the interval $(-6,6),$ and what is the smallest value of $x$ where $f(x)$ has an inflection point?\n\nThere are {{field-1}} \ninflection points in total.\n\nThe inflection point with the smallest $x$-coordinate is at\n\n$x\\,=\\,$ {{field-2}}." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518136 1072 #uuid "51f72acf-5b7c-5f3d-bea9-1b31e5199996" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518136 1074 "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-6,6].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ Recall the following:\n\n* When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$  \n\n* When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ \n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n* ${f}^{″}(a)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(a)$ is undefined, and \n\n* ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nSo, we summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-6,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,6)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the table, we see that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign only at the point $x=0.$  Therefore, $x=0$  is a point of inflection.\n\nFinally, the smallest value of $x$ inside the interval $(-6,6),$ where the function $f(x)$ has an inflection point is $x=0.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518085 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518137 1069 "q-354907" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518137 1071 "Purchasing homemade lemonade costs $$36$ per gallon. What's the unit price per cup?\n\nAssume that there are $16$ cups in one gallon." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518137 1072 #uuid "533e8f29-fadf-5200-8bf2-91c4cb3d254a" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518137 1074 "First, notice that there are $16$ cups in $1$ gallon. So, to find the unit price per cup, we divide the total price by $16:$\n\n$$\n36÷16=2.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, purchasing the lemonade costs $$2.25$ per cup." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518031 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518138 1069 "q-254862" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518138 1071 "Suppose $a=[1,\\,4,\\,-2]^{T}$ and $b=[2,\\,5,\\,3]^{T}.$ Find the Manhattan distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{1}(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518138 1072 #uuid "5380f139-fe32-542e-ba7e-191d7abaa184" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518138 1074 "Recall that the Manhattan distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{1}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{1}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{1}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{1}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{1} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]‖}_{1} \\\\  & =|1-2|+|4-5|+|-2-3| \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517970 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518139 1069 "q-3798" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518139 1071 "Determine the solutions of the equation ${f}^{2}=-25.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518139 1072 #uuid "54e8fcf4-d538-5483-805d-02e6f387fbe1" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518139 1074 "By taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\nf=\\pm \\sqrt{-25}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $f$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f & =\\pm \\sqrt{-25} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{25\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{25}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518078 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518140 1069 "q-254851" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518140 1071 "Suppose $a=[2,\\,6]^{T}$ and $b=[5,\\,3]^{T}.$ Find the Euclidean distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\nRounded to three decimal places, ${d}_{2}(a,b)≈$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518140 1072 #uuid "55bdf128-1307-57a2-a09e-523ed9c58971" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518140 1074 "Recall that the Euclidean distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{2}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{2}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{2} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]‖}_{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{|2-5{|}^{2}+|6-3{|}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & ≈4.243.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518016 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518141 1069 "q-163495" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518141 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518141 1071 "![](images/85/8524ffd2ff653703bd6fcfa2b44d36821aeb34877d58bbc6df388497cac8e07d.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $x$-axis and the lines $y=2x$ and $y=4-2x$, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $y=2.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518141 1072 #uuid "5810c34a-c4d1-5c91-ad48-b47e68d3d961" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518141 1074 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 2-\\frac{y}{2}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/29/293f8454cdda5897de111d6444baa4674eccf28a36e2053f3f2cb71f948e07fe.png)\n\n  \n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $y=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|y-2|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y/2}\\frac{x}{2}|y-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y/2}x(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y/2}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4){[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=y/2}^{x=2-y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[{(2-\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)(4-2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}-2{y}^{3}+12{y}^{2}-24y+16\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}+4{y}^{3}-12{y}^{2}+16y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-8+32-48+32) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518072 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518142 1069 "q-116853" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518142 1071 "If ${f}^{′}(x)=-|2x-5|$ and $f(1)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function  $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518142 1072 #uuid "585b182e-7b17-5dc5-940a-b3dd1c063524" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518142 1074 "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/5f/5f48f6278983a74be84b95984808b23745ab4960dbd427f942efe18df86768e8.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2})$ | $2$ | $(\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical line  $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$ \n\n- Since we are told that $f(1)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(1,\\frac{5}{2}),$ the point at $x=1$ must be higher than the point at $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\n![](images/1f/1ff02109b863615e5274b9ac41306644d8d42ce62671e0e76ecbda6655d249e0.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2})$ and on $(\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left of $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ and goes down to the right of $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=\\frac{5}{2}$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x=\\frac{5}{2}$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/93/931e7763569df18dd0aea8cd1363f6d8414f1a0cc8deddc964ba11a9c74fe127.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/ad/ad5016295fb3d33baac6af6e74dd3b2cbf563c74aef78bf27e5aed28222db3e7.png)" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517991 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518143 1069 "q-20536" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518143 1071 "![](images/3d/3d1a01139fdd6f0f9f996cfce43a1e5ad86c2eabe526e9501f72451bd137f9dd.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(0)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518143 1072 #uuid "5b66ab71-e5ba-5e8c-8d29-3a56ef93e008" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518143 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=0\\,$ and $\\,x=1$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ \n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(0,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be higher than the point at $x=0.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/cf/cfd74974fecdaf62309e089f34f99e0f015584ed3fbb275b9b997c9811c74979.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,0)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(0,1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=0.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(0,1)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(1,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=1.$\n\n![](images/8f/8faca7d91b813d8ce7a964614a19f29d7e3b20d34cf918463151f37bc8f9614b.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/41/4127d1311f92f1e03def285cf922bd5235fd4b2a5ca9614f34e7243c9bf79276.png)" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518012 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518144 1069 "q-337912" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518144 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=5\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(2x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518144 1072 #uuid "5cb35406-f881-5edb-97ef-a6166561d9a4" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518144 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(5\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}\\cdot (2x)^{′} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518040 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518145 1069 "q-20520" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518145 1071 "![](images/3c/3c304ff10a466abe8a7a98342f58d357fec5a46c6971c5dc0b30203bbbb1ec62.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(0)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518145 1072 #uuid "5e28fed1-97b3-526c-8da9-ef22fe1e5ddd" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518145 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-0.5$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-0.5)$ | $-0.5$ | $(-0.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical lines $x=-0.5.$ \n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-0.5,\\infty ),$ the point at $x=-0.5$ must be higher than the point at $x=0.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/9a/9a90c6cc7702720da4fb2619935ce1f663fe09a1f0827c520371d4dc39881cf5.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical point:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-\\infty ,-0.5)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(-0.5,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=-0.5.$\n\n![](images/02/02833ae5c4579ec492812be7bc8e90958108a60cc2f5b7348d4eb6119f667062.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/8f/8ffc84aff1ce37e77b8c0c169a6cb01b9bff7f5f2d27a834ccea251a7d1acbde.png)" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518062 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518146 1069 "q-86308" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518146 1071 "![](images/ab/ab5fb36385ed57a83a8bef13b0e58745fef3454d4e3fae0c54491d80b61637ce.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518146 1072 #uuid "5f23ce06-592e-5661-a235-01cdcf7c430f" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518146 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=3$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=3,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/7e/7eb820e9dedb816318045df9f8dbef2d4b3007446599e56414a803f277acd622.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,3)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(3,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and the right of $x=3.$\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/7d/7dd5014441f5cbe3ba3ee0127feb9cea1f8d264255e1c907ce6d5fdc458aa77f.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/fb/fb709866c18a2ecd61160330bd54c44b481a311e3a1bec589ef26ab8a4c6243f.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518087 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518147 1069 "q-343666" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518147 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+18y=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518147 1072 #uuid "5fd4a0bf-2844-5191-8ab1-3fd5afa7e748" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518147 1074 "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=18\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518148 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518096 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518148 1069 "q-94240" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518148 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518148 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518148 1072 #uuid "6127495a-5135-5ebc-930f-1823afa46170" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518148 1074 "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=3\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{3}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{3}t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518043 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518149 1069 "q-140415" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518149 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3x+12}{{x}^{3}+64}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518149 1072 #uuid "63acba77-7b2e-522a-aba2-29e686142675" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518149 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3x+12}{{x}^{3}+64} & =\\frac{3(-4)+12}{(-4)^{3}+64}=\\frac{-12+12}{-64+64}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. \n\nNotice that the denominator is a sum of cubes. Therefore, factoring our expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+12}{{x}^{3}+64} & =\\frac{3(x+4)}{{x}^{3}+{4}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(x+4)}{(x+4)({x}^{2}-4x+{4}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-4x+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3x+12}{{x}^{3}+64} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-4x+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(-4)^{2}-4(-4)+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16+16+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518007 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518150 1069 "q-2974" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518150 1071 "${log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{1}{27})=$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518150 1072 #uuid "64f9b7ab-1e87-57f9-b089-cc6f9c501397" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518150 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{27}$ as a power of $3$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{27}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}={3}^{-3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{1}{27})={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{-3})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=3$ and $x=-3.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{-3})=-3\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517999 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518151 1069 "q-3800" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518151 1071 "Find the solutions of $-2{d}^{2}-32=0.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518151 1072 #uuid "6745c884-99da-504f-8dea-4d79761c7ad9" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518151 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{d}^{2}-32 & =0 \\\\ -2{d}^{2} & =32 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\nd=\\pm \\sqrt{-16}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $d$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\pm \\sqrt{-16} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{16\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518004 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518152 1069 "q-81038" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518152 1071 "If ${f}^{′}(x)=-|x-5|,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518152 1072 #uuid "67f4106c-b68b-5844-893c-27d541fb64bf" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518152 1074 "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/54/54c03ba5ea869394d9b3d35c64bb012a8c5f9917a5ebb96fd67994ace6a84d4f.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=5$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,5)$ | $5$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=5,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/25/25d067dcff3b0cc4b88859a318023288a7c9786b5c73910f2addf3952680596b.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,5)$ and on $(5,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left of $x=5$ and goes down to the right of $x=5.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=5$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x=5$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, $x=5$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/b7/b7b1e5915931501d881ba5efc1aaccb8af3a604ac397ac662d8bb7811659c17a.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/cb/cb451ce921bb8bc56576545b4503bf3dd44bb1ee070466576437c2ce980dd017.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517992 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518153 1069 "q-68552" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518153 1071 "At a convenience store, a $5$-pound canister of licorice candy costs $$11.20.$ What's the unit price per ounce?\n\nAssume that there are $16$ ounces in one pound." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518153 1072 #uuid "694684ae-3bf6-514c-9bb8-aef47c5c19c7" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518153 1074 "First, we convert from pounds to ounces. There are $16$ ounces in each pound, so we multiply the number of pounds  by $16:$  \n\n$$\n5\\times 16=80\n$$\n\nSo, $5$ pounds is the same as $80$ ounces. Now, to find the unit price per ounce, we divide the total price by $80:$  \n\n$$\n11.20÷80=0.14\n$$\n\nTherefore, the licorice candy costs $$0.14$ per ounce." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517982 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518154 1069 "q-26042" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518154 1071 "${log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{1}{81})=$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518154 1072 #uuid "6c887066-3c08-5cb4-b40e-433eb14c4b31" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518154 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{81}$ as a power of $3$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{81}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}}={3}^{-4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{1}{81})={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{-4})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=3$ and $x=-4.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{-4})=-4\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517972 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518155 1069 "q-86540" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518155 1071 "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,-1,0)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518155 1072 #uuid "6d63b608-6564-54eb-94c2-8afc07416275" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518155 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,-\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,8z⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,-1,0) & =⟨\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,-\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,8\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517984 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518156 1069 "q-95515" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518156 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,x),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518156 1072 #uuid "70dcb121-2c51-5a9a-a8d4-5a609e0f6393" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518156 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,x)) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,x)) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-{(\\sqrt{6}\\,x)}^{2}}\\cdot {(\\sqrt{6}\\,x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-6{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{1-6{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518028 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518157 1069 "q-98343" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518157 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518157 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(3,0,4)=-5,\\,{f}_{x}(3,0,4)=3,\\,{f}_{y}(3,0,4)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(3,0,4)=2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,0,4)$ estimate the value of $f(2.97,-0.02,3.98).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518157 1072 #uuid "72ddfe60-c39c-529d-b7e5-b5ef0ab41db5" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518157 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,0,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,0,4)+∇f(3,0,4)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-0,z-4⟩ \\\\  & =f(3,0,4)+⟨{f}_{x}(3,0,4),{f}_{y}(3,0,4),{f}_{z}(3,0,4)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y,z-4⟩ \\\\  & =-5+⟨3,-1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y,z-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.97,-0.02,3.98)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=2.97,$ $y=-0.02,$ and $z=3.98$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.97,-0.02,3.98) & ≈-5+⟨3,-1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2.97-3,-0.02,3.98-4⟩ \\\\  & =-5+⟨3,-1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.03,-0.02,-0.02⟩ \\\\  & =-5-0.09+0.02-0.04 \\\\  & =-5.11\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518063 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518158 1069 "q-342834" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518158 1071 "$$\n\nCalculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}  $-\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518158 1072 #uuid "75d28ef3-1631-5b9b-9daa-067de055774b" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518158 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAnd the quotient rule is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}(\\frac{u}{v})}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, chain rule, and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2})}^{′}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-{({x}^{2})}^{2}}\\cdot {({x}^{2})}^{′}-\\frac{\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{4}}\\cdot 2x-\\frac{\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(1-{x}^{4})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518036 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518159 1069 "q-112361" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518159 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518159 1071 "If $\\frac{{w}^{2}}{4}+22=0,$ then $w=$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518159 1072 #uuid "77b8b796-0079-55bf-a33c-a1e9a651dc45" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518159 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{w}^{2}}{4}+22 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{w}^{2}}{4} & =-22 \\\\ {w}^{2} & =-88\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\nw=\\pm \\sqrt{-88}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $w$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =\\pm \\sqrt{-88} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{88\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{88}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{22}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{22}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518160 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518030 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518160 1069 "q-79024" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518160 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518160 1071 "What is the area of a circle that has a diameter of $4?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518160 1072 #uuid "78884415-9ec2-5003-9971-d3d9da1f23e6" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518160 1074 "The radius of a circle is half the diameter. So, the radius of our circle is $r=2.$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518013 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518161 1069 "q-254909" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518161 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518161 1071 "Suppose $a=[7,\\,6,\\,-2]^{T}$ and $b=[-1,\\,2,\\,4]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518161 1072 #uuid "78d962fb-98a1-54a9-be40-612e78bd3a7f" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518161 1074 "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|7-(-1)|,|6-2|,|-2-4|) \\\\  & =max(8,4,6) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518008 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518162 1069 "q-254915" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518162 1071 "Suppose $a=[0,\\,-6]^{T}$ and $b=[0.5,\\,-7]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518162 1072 #uuid "79a48270-e1b9-5009-9c26-f6b63d86b07c" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518162 1074 "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|0-0.5|,|-6-(-7)|) \\\\  & =max(0.5,1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517975 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518163 1069 "q-254910" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518163 1071 "Suppose $a=[-3,\\,5,\\,1]^{T}$ and $b=[2,\\,7,\\,4]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518163 1072 #uuid "7ac6fcb3-d9b4-5799-8b69-524dd3ce6133" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518163 1074 "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|-3-2|,|5-7|,|1-4|) \\\\  & =max(5,2,3) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518038 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518164 1069 "q-20538" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518164 1071 "![](images/ef/ef23af714377518200c574ba436a30001ed5f60262e11f8157f62d49cbec317e.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(0)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518164 1072 #uuid "7bca001f-f1ef-5c7b-838d-dca97d1b4371" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518164 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are three critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-3,$ $x=-2,\\,$ and $\\,x=-1.$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=-3,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=-1.$\n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-1,0)$, the point at $x=-1$ must be below the $x$-axis.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-2,-1)$, the point at $x=-2$ must be higher than the point at $x=-1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-3,-2)$, the point at $x=-3$ must be lower than the point at $x=-2.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/66/66296cbca22d3059fba111935808d1bb0a78e9de31c45948fce207d998c9cfd2.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-3,-2)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-3.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-3,-2)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(-2,-1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=-2.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-2,-1)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-1,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n![](images/b2/b26af4cdc5af02f2cf0a9a0092f0fe908d70acd428e6a1e24bc848bcc99125e4.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/93/938c86a415620a2e80873f7cee72b2ebaf5579ae2e67eb966bb1f8836e62c087.png)" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517977 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518165 1069 "q-254852" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518165 1071 "Suppose $a=[4,\\,6]^{T}$ and $b=[2,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Find the Euclidean distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\nRounded to three decimal places, ${d}_{2}(a,b)≈$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518165 1072 #uuid "7c744e92-6824-57b8-b5de-89c3c18659be" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518165 1074 "Recall that the Euclidean distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{2}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{2}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{2} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]‖}_{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{|4-2{|}^{2}+|6-(-2){|}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{68} \\\\  & ≈8.246.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518027 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518166 1069 "q-344305" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518166 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡4{x}^{6},$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | $8{x}^{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518166 1072 #uuid "7e33df1c-2868-500a-90eb-4948dc41205c" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518166 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡4{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡4{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(4{x}^{6})}^{2}+1}}\\cdot {(4{x}^{6})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{12}+1}}\\cdot 24{x}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{12}+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517990 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518167 1069 "q-338206" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518167 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{7{y}^{6}-3{y}^{4}}{5{y}^{5}+10{y}^{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518167 1072 #uuid "7eccf962-783f-535d-b46e-1c5922c7413f" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518167 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{7{y}^{6}-3{y}^{4}}{5{y}^{5}+10{y}^{4}} & =\\frac{7(0)^{6}-3(0)^{4}}{5(0)^{5}+10(0)^{4}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7{y}^{6}-3{y}^{4}}{5{y}^{5}+10{y}^{4}} & =\\frac{{y}^{4}(7{y}^{2}-3)}{{y}^{4}(5y+10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{7{y}^{2}-3}{5y+10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{7{y}^{6}-3{y}^{4}}{5{y}^{5}+10{y}^{4}} & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{7{y}^{2}-3}{5y+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{7(0)-3}{5(0)+10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518019 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518052 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518168 1069 "q-131251" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518168 1071 "Given that $f(-3)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2},$ find the absolute minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,6].$\n\n${x}_{\\text{min}}\\,=\\,$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,f({x}_{\\text{min}})\\,=\\,$  \n{{field-2}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518168 1072 #uuid "7f985c78-b1b4-50b7-b3f8-18827e16fba5" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518168 1074 "The minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on $[-6,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\nTo find the minimum value, we apply three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points. From the graph, we see that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ and the endpoints of the interval are $x=-6,6.$ So, the candidates are $x=-6,\\,-3,\\,3,\\,6.$ \n\n**Step 2**: Discard any candidates that could not be a minimum.\n\n* The derivative is negative on $(-6,-3),$ so $f(x)$ is decreasing on that interval, and therefore $f(-6)>f(-3).$ So, $x=-6$ cannot be a minimum. \n\n* The derivative is positive on $(-3,3),$ so $f(x)$ is increasing on that interval, and therefore $f(-3)<f(3).$ So, $x=3$ cannot be a minimum. \n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the values of $f(x)$ at the remaining candidates. We have discarded $x=-6,\\,3,$ so the remaining candidates are $x=-3,\\,6.$\n\n* We are given that $f(-3)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$\n\n* Previously, we found that $f(6)=2\\pi -6.$\n\nSo, comparing $f(-3)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}≈-7.85$ and $f(6)=2\\pi -6≈0.28,$ we conclude that the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,6]$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2},$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-3.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518064 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518169 1069 "q-60254" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518169 1071 "![](images/a3/a37bbb00bcf37dce19970f5494412eaa9c68e5db134354e3c9d790cd9dc7a5b5.png)\n\nWhat ratio describes the number of triangles to squares?" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518169 1072 #uuid "7fc0e3a0-04f8-58fa-8d72-05025f5c40e7" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518169 1074 "We count the number of triangles. We see that there is $1$ triangle.\n\n We count the number of square. We see that there are $3$ squares.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to squares is $\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518021 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518170 1069 "q-342186" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518170 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡4{x}^{6},$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | $40{x}^{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518170 1072 #uuid "81194d8a-eb61-596d-a974-e9c1b95b27d0" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518170 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡4{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡4{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(4{x}^{6})}^{2}-1}}\\cdot {(4{x}^{6})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{12}-1}}\\cdot 24{x}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{40{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{12}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518067 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518171 1069 "q-254864" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518171 1071 "Suppose $a=[1,\\,4,\\,-2]^{T}$ and $b=[-4,\\,-1,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Find the Manhattan distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{1}(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518171 1072 #uuid "83af6a91-b190-50f3-bc76-025dc5091d7d" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518171 1074 "Recall that the Manhattan distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{1}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{1}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{1}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{1}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{1} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]‖}_{1} \\\\  & =|1-(-4)|+|4-(-1)|+|-2-(-2)| \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517988 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518172 1069 "q-60256" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518172 1071 "![](images/06/06931fae92045635d523614dce24063856209b1f04987471b9de183a3ccce4a2.png)\n\nIf the ratio of rhombuses to total shapes is $\\frac{3}{\\boxed{}}$ then what is the missing number?" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518172 1072 #uuid "8c4f25cf-0173-54ec-98c6-ab10b632fa9a" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518172 1074 "We count the number of rhombuses. We see that there are $3$ rhombuses.\n\n We count the total number of shapes. We see that there are $7$ shapes.\n\nHence, the ratio of circles to triangles is$\\frac{3}{7}$\n\nSo the missing number is $7.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517994 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518173 1069 "q-98376" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518173 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\frac{7}{10}$ is a rational number\n\nII. $-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a rational number\n\nIII. $3$ is a rational number" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518173 1072 #uuid "8c89a512-5fc0-599a-9173-8df36d4b9b39" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518173 1074 "A rational number is any number that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $\\frac{7}{10}$ is a rational number.\n\n- $-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a rational number. It can be written as $\\frac{(-5)}{3}.$\n\n- $3$ is a rational number. It can be written as $\\frac{3}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518073 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518174 1069 "q-10741" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518174 1071 "If $y=\\frac{11}{{x}^{9}},$ find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518174 1072 #uuid "8ce73f5e-c8df-5814-9aaf-e78dee12c64a" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518174 1074 "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{11}{{x}^{9}}=11{x}^{-9}.\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(11{x}^{-9}) \\\\  & =11\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-9}) \\\\  & =11\\cdot (-9){x}^{-9-1} \\\\  & =-99{x}^{-10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{99}{{x}^{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518175 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518002 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518175 1069 "q-43872" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518175 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518175 1071 "Which of the following is **not** an algebraic expression?\n\nI. $4+2y$\n\nII. $x+\\sqrt{5}$\n\nIII. $7-4=3$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518175 1072 #uuid "8e57b39f-05a9-5f35-9efb-5ed20831848c" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518175 1074 "III is **not** an algebraic expression since it contains the equality sign." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518092 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518176 1069 "q-66588" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518176 1071 "${log}_{4}⁡64=$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518176 1072 #uuid "8fd2dd92-c19f-59ac-abcb-f54b4019b8cc" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518176 1074 "First, we rewrite $64$ as a power of $4$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n64={4}^{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{log}_{4}⁡64={log}_{4}⁡({4}^{3})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=4$ and $x=3.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{4}⁡({4}^{3})=3\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517976 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518177 1069 "q-354914" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518177 1071 "Sarah bought $1$ ounce of almonds and paid $$0.75$ for it. What's the unit price per pound?\n\nAssume that there are $16$ ounces in one pound." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518177 1072 #uuid "924bf994-7977-5b78-af84-c2d13087032d" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518177 1074 "First, notice that there are $16$ ounces in each pound.  \n\nSince the price is $$0.75$ per ounce, to find the price per pound, we multiply by $16:$\n\n$$\n0.75\\times 16=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the almonds cost $$12$ per pound." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517985 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518178 1069 "q-96442" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518178 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(3x-1),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518178 1072 #uuid "96d64ea4-0cd9-5375-8219-9bae598e5ccf" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518178 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(3x-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(3x-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-{(3x-1)}^{2}}\\cdot {(3x-1)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-(9{x}^{2}-6x+1)}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6x-9{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x(2-3x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518079 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518179 1069 "q-86431" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518179 1071 "If ${f}^{′}(x)=-|x+1|,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518179 1072 #uuid "9fc26721-45c6-5872-bde4-819ab63691ac" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518179 1074 "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/c4/c4e03ef210691e4eee922d0c35e90abb9cde1a78064ad491d4ac084d875c4a57.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/40/40b5e9b8928be63a415877c0633f0302c874db51b1c74a1f82b7b1526a6f4d14.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and is decreasing $↘$ on $(-1,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left of $x=-1$ and goes down to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=-1$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x=-1$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, $x=-1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/c6/c68544957f33afdeab436a3fbf75b9e27990a2e7f3ea75f39c6e58dce24f6185.png)\n\n \n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/4c/4c91ba8f06642fe14035400efa61dbd2d8bbadd07274fedc9a90678970bbb9a8.png)\n\n \n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518037 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518180 1069 "q-254843" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518180 1071 "Suppose $a=[2,\\,1,\\,4]^{T}$ and $b=[1,\\,3,\\,5]^{T}.$ Find the Euclidean distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\nRounded to three decimal places, ${d}_{2}(a,b)≈$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518180 1072 #uuid "a4985131-38a1-553b-8a59-bf4137759de2" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518180 1074 "Recall that the Euclidean distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{2}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{2}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{2} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]‖}_{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{|2-1{|}^{2}+|1-3{|}^{2}+|4-5{|}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6} \\\\  & ≈2.449.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517993 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518181 1069 "q-26049" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518181 1071 "Calculate ${log}_{7}⁡({7}^{2/5})$ without using a calculator." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518181 1072 #uuid "a77a6bbf-206b-5fc8-b45f-b94bd7cd1684" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518181 1074 "The logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=7$ and $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{7}⁡({7}^{2/5})=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518017 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518182 1069 "q-102534" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518182 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{17-2x}+3.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518182 1072 #uuid "a9239051-66d6-5587-8b77-ad2324021796" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518182 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[5]{17-2x}+3=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[5]{17-2x}+3 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[5]{17-2x} & =-3 \\\\ (\\sqrt[5]{17-2x})^{5} & =(-3)^{5} \\\\ 17-2x & =-243 \\\\ -2x & =-260 \\\\ x & =130\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=130.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518086 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518183 1069 "q-171153" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518183 1071 "$0÷(\\frac{2}{3})=${{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518183 1072 #uuid "a98d2b92-1f79-5299-a15c-071e314b9a2c" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518183 1074 "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore, $0÷(\\frac{2}{3})=0.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518005 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518184 1069 "q-254904" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518184 1071 "Suppose $a=[1,\\,1,\\,-1]^{T},$ and $b=[2,\\,-1,\\,-2]^{T},$ and $p=3.$ Find the Minkowski distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\nRounded to three decimal places, ${d}_{p}(a,b)≈$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518184 1072 #uuid "ae95185c-810c-50b4-b278-d16c314036aa" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518184 1074 "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{p}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{p}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{p}={({\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{p})}^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{3}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{3} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]‖}_{3} \\\\  & ={(|1-2{|}^{3}+|1-(-1){|}^{3}+|-1-(-2){|}^{3})}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={10}^{1/3} \\\\  & ≈2.154.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518076 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518185 1069 "q-171905" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518185 1071 "![](images/bd/bd6b42e1d48ca0e45ffc9b8910345bea52f1f44f9754f00ac4a4ba034e273bb5.png)\n\nGiven the quadrilateral above, we have $x=${{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518185 1072 #uuid "b40960a4-3462-5880-9ff6-2c1313bf33f8" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518185 1074 "Since the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3x+(2x+{20}^{∘})+(5x-{24}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 14x-{4}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 14x & ={364}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={26}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518020 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518186 1069 "q-354900" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518186 1071 "Buying organic milk costs $$14.40$ per gallon. What's the unit price per quart?\n\nAssume that there are $4$ quarts in one gallon." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518186 1072 #uuid "b6d906ea-b787-55f0-a205-6b6f9a337939" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518186 1074 "First, notice that there are $4$ quarts in $1$ gallon. So, to find the unit price per quart, we divide the total price by $4:$\n\n$$\n14.40÷4=3.60\n$$\n\nTherefore, the milk costs $$3.60$ per quart." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518060 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518187 1069 "q-85435" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518187 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $26\\,489\\,225$\n\nII. $14\\,736\\,438$\n\nIII. $749\\,356\\,314$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518187 1072 #uuid "bd6001aa-5beb-57ae-855c-a9db7b063e64" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518187 1074 "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$\n\n- The number $26\\,489\\,225$ is not divisible by $2,$ so it is not divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- The number $14\\,736\\,438$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n1+4+7+3+6+4+3+8=36\n$$\n\nSo, $14\\,736\\,438$ is divisible by $6.$\n\n- The number $749\\,356\\,314$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n7+4+9+3+5+6+3+1+4=42\n$$\n\nSo, $749\\,356\\,314$ is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518080 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518188 1069 "q-59898" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518188 1071 "![](images/ec/ec8c3585db1981d001864b3c135f84e46643051d22f28e77f82e8b5a92d0f09d.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of squares to circles is $\\boxed{}:1$ because there are $\\boxed{}$ squares for every $1$ circle." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518188 1072 #uuid "c42ab941-f231-5a97-a430-88c5273648a5" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518188 1074 "First, we count the number of squares. We see that there are $3$ squares.\n\nThen, we count the number of circles. We see that there are $3$ circles.\n\nSo the ratio of squares to circles is $3:3.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $3:3.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of squares to circles is $1:1$ because there is $1$ square for every $1$ circle.\"" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518022 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518189 1069 "q-109030" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518189 1071 "Which of the following shows the point $D(-5,4)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518189 1072 #uuid "c49fa7b3-27b5-573b-aadb-233575243dcc" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518189 1074 "Starting at the origin, we move along the $x$ and $y$ axes to get to the point $D.$\n\nFirst, we move along the $x$-axis. The point $D$ has an $x$-coordinate of $-5,$ so we move $5$ units to the *left*, in the negative direction of the $x$-axis.\n\nThen, we move along the $y$-axis. The point $D$ has a $y$-coordinate of $4,$ so we move $4$ units *up*, in the positive direction of the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/19/19520b88504572a892e70dc3a40d16a3635c8528f9e591ac82aa0411d8091129.png)" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518081 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518190 1069 "q-66579" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518190 1071 "${log}_{10}⁡({10}^{4})=$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518190 1072 #uuid "c63b77a8-effd-51a8-a515-1fb5e14ee3e6" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518190 1074 "The logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=10$ and $x=4.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{10}⁡({10}^{4})=4.\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518049 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518191 1069 "q-108074" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518191 1071 "${log}_{2}⁡(256)=$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518191 1072 #uuid "c67b84ce-63b0-5172-a4ca-d019c33deb20" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518191 1074 "First, we rewrite $256$ as a power of $2$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n256={2}^{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡256={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{8})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=2$ and $x=8.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{8})=8\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517979 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518192 1069 "q-254863" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518192 1071 "Suppose $a=[3,\\,5,\\,1]^{T}$ and $b=[4,\\,-1,\\,6]^{T}.$ Find the Manhattan distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{1}(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518192 1072 #uuid "c6fc9a2a-2dca-5aed-a889-2bc4c863a299" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518192 1074 "Recall that the Manhattan distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{1}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{1}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{1}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{1}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{1} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]‖}_{1} \\\\  & =|3-4|+|5-(-1)|+|1-6| \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518025 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518193 1069 "q-99678" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518193 1071 "${log}_{49}⁡1=$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518193 1072 #uuid "c8c27c46-9870-56fc-a5a5-2b4126a5a87a" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518193 1074 "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡1=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=49.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{49}⁡1=0\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518003 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518194 1069 "q-254858" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518194 1071 "Suppose $a=[1,\\,-5]^{T}$ and $b=[5,\\,1]^{T}.$ Find the Manhattan distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{1}(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518194 1072 #uuid "c95e267e-85b4-5649-968c-53ba871cff30" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518194 1074 "Recall that the Manhattan distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{1}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{1}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{1}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{1}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{1} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]‖}_{1} \\\\  & =|1-5|+|-5-1| \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517998 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518195 1069 "q-163668" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518195 1071 "![](images/de/de1ac9ccf84134913bbf8847ea9795a1ec83084602db705c8bef723897e7bd92.png)\n\nA triangular plate with mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed between the axes and the line $y=1-x.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $y$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x=3.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518195 1072 #uuid "c9bc333f-6b0c-5f6d-9c9d-d8671bcc2662" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518195 1074 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a092d57585bd7b2803473c30328dd72f82a6611d8398de821099db72e2ed3621.png)\n\nAlso, the distance between a point and the line $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-3|.\n$$\n\nNext, the mass density can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k|x|=kx,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find the value of $k$ by integrating the mass density function over the plate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}kx\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}x[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{[y]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}x(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}x-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=kx=6Mx.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}6Mx\\,|x-3{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}6Mx(x-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6M{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x)[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6M{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x){[y]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6M{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x)(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6M{[-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}+\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6M[-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{7}{4}-5+\\frac{9}{2}-0] \\\\  & =6M[\\frac{21}{20}] \\\\  & =\\frac{63M}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518068 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518196 1069 "q-338200" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518196 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{y\\to 4}\\frac{{y}^{2}-16}{{y}^{2}-4y}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518196 1072 #uuid "c9bfaabb-1ddb-5591-87c5-e59f3a52dfbc" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518196 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 4}\\frac{{y}^{2}-16}{{y}^{2}-4y} & =\\frac{(4)^{2}-16}{(4)^{2}-4(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{16-16}{16-16} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}-16}{{y}^{2}-4y} & =\\frac{(y-4)(y+4)}{y(y-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{y+4}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 4}\\frac{{y}^{2}-16}{{y}^{2}-4y} & ={lim}_{y\\to 4}\\frac{y+4}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517969 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518197 1069 "q-66250" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518197 1071 "If ${y}^{2}+63=0,$ then $y=$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518197 1072 #uuid "cd11ce23-27fe-5c7c-9502-a02ca58513ca" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518197 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+63 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\sqrt{-63}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $y$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\sqrt{-63} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{63\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{63}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518015 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518198 1069 "q-254889" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518198 1071 "Suppose $a=[3,\\,1]^{T},$ and $b=[2,\\,2]^{T},$ and $p=5.$ Find the Minkowski distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\nRounded to three decimal places, ${d}_{p}(a,b)≈$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518198 1072 #uuid "cd8b6d86-5eba-5e13-9d83-5bea3e076943" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518198 1074 "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{p}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{p}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{p}={({\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{p})}^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{5}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{5} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]‖}_{5} \\\\  & ={(|3-2{|}^{5}+|1-2{|}^{5})}^{1/5} \\\\  & ={2}^{1/5} \\\\  & ≈1.149.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518039 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518199 1069 "q-65339" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518199 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518199 1072 #uuid "cf3c18cd-d64f-5eb9-99df-fc0a97727985" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518199 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)+∇f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{2},y-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & ={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\, & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)=(2)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2)=-4, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2)=-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2) & =⟨-4,-\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)+∇\\,f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,y-2⟩ \\\\  & =0+⟨-4,-\\pi ⟩\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,y-2⟩ \\\\  & =-4(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi (y-2) \\\\  & =4\\pi -4x-\\pi y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518069 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518200 1069 "q-115589" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518200 1071 "![](images/96/9674072fe2d9f831635dfd2994b97ea6e9603dd576586f80e4b1b2e6a2373564.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the relationship between $x$ and $y.$ Given that $y$ is inversely proportional to $x,$ find the value of $a.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518200 1072 #uuid "cf708341-904e-56d2-b929-e74c203d19a7" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518200 1074 "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nIn the graph, we notice a point with $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=5.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{k}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5 & =k \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse variation equation that relates $y$ and $x$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{2})}{x}=\\frac{5}{2x}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we also have that $y=\\frac{5}{2}$ when $x=a.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{5}{2a} \\\\ 10a & =10 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518041 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518201 1069 "q-345246" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518201 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{4}(t)=u(t-4)$ is the unit step function." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518201 1072 #uuid "d2f59173-bdb2-59d3-8bec-268e720e4bf5" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518201 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}+16y\\} & =L\\{7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+16L\\{y\\} & =L\\{7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the second derivative and the initial conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=3:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-3\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=4$ and $f(t)=7(t-4).$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{7u(t-4)\\cdot (t-4)\\} & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot L\\{7((t+4)-4)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot L\\{7t\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot 7\\cdot \\frac{1!}{{s}^{1+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+16L\\{y\\} & =L\\{7{u}_{4}(t)(t-4)\\} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s)-3+16Y(s) & =\\frac{7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+16)Y(s) & =3+\\frac{7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+16)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518023 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518202 1069 "q-254842" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518202 1071 "Suppose $a=[-1,\\,1,\\,0]^{T}$ and $b=[2,\\,3,\\,1]^{T}.$ Find the Euclidean distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\nRounded to three decimal places, ${d}_{2}(a,b)≈$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518202 1072 #uuid "d6227e18-efad-598b-bec8-32e2a28de5a3" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518202 1074 "Recall that the Euclidean distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{2}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{2}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{2} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]‖}_{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{|-1-2{|}^{2}+|1-3{|}^{2}+|0-1{|}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\  & ≈3.742.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518006 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518203 1069 "q-51330" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518203 1071 "![](images/9e/9e7c8d7f80211aebbd8a8fb5456e2bfe0a5dd0dc46addfe65777eb09f0b4c66a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518203 1072 #uuid "d823907c-c12e-50eb-a396-a258624fbd1c" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518203 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:  \n\n$\\,$ $x=3$  \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical lines $x=3$ and choose an arbitrary point on it.\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/4a/4aa4592a6edae5207c7e934aa148e87dd8a8762f6bbdba260cc137bd64b407b9.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(2,3)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(3,\\infty )$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=3.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is a positive constant to the left of $x=2$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ represents a section of an increasing  straight line on $(-\\infty ,2).$ Also, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing to the right of $x=2$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave down on $(2,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/34/347dda820c8307984b88ffb5f2b528c20984ec8a7e9acbe9b7f3d8f457a917b2.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/99/99de5224b614ea11b454020d7a165b58ff0fcf47708faba9973255276133eebc.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518095 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518204 1069 "q-24414" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518204 1071 "${log}_{10}⁡(10)=$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518204 1072 #uuid "d8d5b32c-2799-58e2-987c-6d259f700464" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518204 1074 "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡n=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=10.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{10}⁡10=1\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518055 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518205 1069 "q-223237" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518205 1071 "![](images/4c/4c8bc8341b9619e9fddb151b02d2d859fab801fe35114de6b2ce6d09045fff11.png)\n\nFor the quadrilateral above, the measure of the exterior angle of $∠C$ is{{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518205 1072 #uuid "da26be71-f6d6-5898-b94d-3e43f1a50c61" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518205 1074 "Note that the measure of the exterior angle at $B$ is ${180}^{∘}-(4x+{18}^{∘})={162}^{∘}-4x.$\n\nSince the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x+{15}^{∘})+({162}^{∘}-4x)+(4x-{2}^{∘})+(3x+{1}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 8x+{176}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 8x & ={184}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={23}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of the exterior angle of $∠C$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-{2}^{∘} & =4({23}^{∘})-{2}^{∘} \\\\  & ={92}^{∘}-{2}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518011 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518206 1069 "q-345300" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518206 1071 "Find the solution $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)+6{y}^{′}(t)+5y(t)=20{u}_{4}(t),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)+6sY(s)+5Y(s)=\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s}.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=u(t-4)\\cdot (4-5{e}^{-(t-4)}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518206 1072 #uuid "e0f1706f-269a-5658-8fd3-75993082b4c8" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518206 1074 "Using the transformed equation, we solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+6sY(s)+5Y(s) & =\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+6s+5)Y(s) & =\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\ (s+1)(s+5)Y(s) & =\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{20}{s(s+1)(s+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first use partial fraction decomposition on the algebraic part:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{s(s+1)(s+5)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{s+1}+\\frac{C}{s+5}\n$$\n\nSolving for the constants, we find $A=4,$ $B=-5,$ and $C=1,$ giving:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{s(s+1)(s+5)}=\\frac{4}{s}-\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{s+5}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the inverse Laplace transform. Let \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{4}{s}-\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{s+5},\n$$\n\nthen, we have\n\n$$\nf(t)=4-5{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the second shifting theorem, \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}={u}_{a}(t)f(t-a).\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-4s}F(s)\\} \\\\ y(t) & ={u}_{4}(t)\\,f(t-4) \\\\ y(t) & =u(t-4)\\cdot (4-5{e}^{-(t-4)}+{e}^{-5(t-4)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518061 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518207 1069 "q-354645" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518207 1071 "Maria bought $1$ cup of olive oil and paid $$1.50$ for it. What's the unit price per gallon?\n\nAssume that there are $16$ cups in one gallon." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518207 1072 #uuid "e1dac973-b461-5ce9-a7ae-b3034ec42db1" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518207 1074 "First, notice that there are $16$ cups in each gallon.  \n\nSince the price is $$1.50$ per cup, to find the price per gallon, we multiply by $16:$\n\n$$\n1.50\\times 16=24\n$$\n\nTherefore, the olive oil costs $$24$ per gallon." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518001 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518208 1069 "q-254913" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518208 1071 "Suppose $a=[5,\\,3]^{T}$ and $b=[9,\\,-9]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518208 1072 #uuid "e36e927d-aa55-5840-81c8-aa7b916f3c2f" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518208 1074 "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|5-9|,|3-(-9)|) \\\\  & =max(4,12) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518009 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518209 1069 "q-223198" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518209 1071 "![](images/a9/a92c3df4f9d410b1a7620cf43b04fda1ce7312cebd8c6077a0b0411ecc78ad73.png)\n\nGiven the quadrilateral above, we have $x=${{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518209 1072 #uuid "e54967a4-313f-5425-b80c-928ee57bb212" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518209 1074 "Since the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2x+(x+{10}^{∘})+(2x-{10}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 6x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518210 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517978 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518210 1069 "q-133671" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518210 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518210 1071 "Given that $a$ is an **irrational** number, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is irrational\n\nII. $a-\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is always rational\n\nIII. $a+\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is sometimes rational and sometimes irrational" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518210 1072 #uuid "e5ec21fc-d95b-5a6e-8483-328c912290e8" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518210 1074 "Recall that: \n\n- the sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $-\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is rational and $a$ is irrational, their sum or difference is always an irrational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518045 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518211 1069 "q-108073" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518211 1071 "${log}_{8}⁡({8}^{7})=$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518211 1072 #uuid "e61eca8e-14ab-54c4-ad94-08eb1910feb8" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518211 1074 "The logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=8$ and $x=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{log}_{8}⁡({8}^{7})=7.\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517973 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518212 1069 "q-20519" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518212 1071 "![](images/a5/a53074660204fdd4fd62575ef8db80a3b04144ef7b9e18d98c60fbbe9a480ee3.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518212 1072 #uuid "e89c21de-ead1-56e3-b348-ff522b66f5d8" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518212 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1\\,$ and $\\,x=1$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical lines $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-1,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be higher than the point at $x=-1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/12/12e6a8036759b8bbcc2f4fbe4ae7f4f7e18278de65fbdc0faa99c919f7234284.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and is increasing $↗$ on $(-1,1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-1,1)$ and is decreasing $↘$ on $(1,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and the right of $x=1.$\n\n![](images/38/38f1c92810a3311ed939a54ac2856cb7ada111dbf24528785f80bd5f9ca49c84.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/79/7921a107b33a9b9bdc3df8dec418828f4cd5de2d89a9e7ab41845b0c64bbb653.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518071 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518213 1069 "q-131250" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518213 1071 "Find the value of $x\\in (-6,6)$ where the function $f(x)$ has a relative maximum.\n\nThe relative maximum is at $x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518213 1072 #uuid "e8c45a53-973c-5ec6-9492-ec10ad530490" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518213 1074 "The function $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=a$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n* ${f}^{′}(a)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n* ${f}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign from positive to negative (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-6,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,6)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=3$ only." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517986 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518214 1069 "q-342829" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518214 1071 "$$\n\nCalculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=7\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}\\,+$ | $7{e}^{x}\\sqrt{x}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518214 1072 #uuid "ea0760db-381e-5949-ad3f-4c6362ce39c8" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518214 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAnd the product rule is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}(u\\cdot v)}{\\text{d}x}={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, chain rule, and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={(7\\sqrt{x})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}+7\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\text{arsinh}\\,({e}^{x})+7\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({e}^{x})}^{2}+1}}\\cdot {({e}^{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}+7\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}+1}}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}+\\frac{7{e}^{x}\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}+1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517971 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518215 1069 "q-1866" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518215 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=3{x}^{4}+\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}}-12x.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518215 1072 #uuid "ec8a3447-04a1-55a0-a68b-6b9044cae34d" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518215 1074 "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}+\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}}-12x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{-4})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12x) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4})+7\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-4})-12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4{x}^{(4-1)}+7\\cdot (-4){x}^{(-4-1)}-12\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}-28{x}^{-5}-12 \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}-\\frac{28}{{x}^{5}}-12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518093 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518216 1069 "q-59903" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518216 1071 "There are $15$ girls and $9$ boys in a geometry class. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of girls to boys is $5:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $5$ girls for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ boys." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518216 1072 #uuid "f0e3ac9f-3533-517a-898b-d1cc891d8b27" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518216 1074 "Since there are $15$ girls and $9$ boys, the ratio of girls to boys is $15:9.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,15 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,5 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $5:3$ is equivalent to $15:9.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of girls to boys is $5:3\\,$ because there are $5$ girls for every $3\\,$ boys.\"\n\nHence, the missing number is $3.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518066 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518217 1069 "q-108076" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518217 1071 "Calculate ${log}_{72}⁡(72).$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518217 1072 #uuid "f14ba22e-b6ea-5533-a5ad-bd5ce2cfcd84" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518217 1074 "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡n=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=72.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{72}⁡72=1\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518044 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518218 1069 "q-130272" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518218 1071 "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(0,1,2)?$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518218 1072 #uuid "f39e417c-6152-5bd7-a05f-5fd83a9d6cdc" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518218 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{z}^{2},\\,-3{y}^{2},\\,2xz⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1,2) & =⟨{2}^{2},-3(1)^{2},2(0)(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨4,-3,0⟩.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518077 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518219 1069 "q-254857" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518219 1071 "Suppose $a=[7,\\,4]^{T}$ and $b=[3,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Find the Manhattan distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{1}(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518219 1072 #uuid "f3e294b9-f845-51ae-87f3-5926acf855f0" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518219 1074 "Recall that the Manhattan distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{1}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{1}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{1}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{1}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{1} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]‖}_{1} \\\\  & =|7-3|+|4-(-2)| \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518053 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518220 1069 "q-254905" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518220 1071 "Suppose $a=[2,\\,-1,\\,1]^{T},$ and $b=[3,\\,0,\\,2]^{T},$ and $p=6.$ Find the Minkowski distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\nRounded to three decimal places, ${d}_{p}(a,b)≈$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518220 1072 #uuid "f4c50ce1-0092-5528-9c12-822dbb56b8d9" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518220 1074 "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{p}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{p}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{p}={({\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{p})}^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{6}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{6} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]‖}_{6} \\\\  & ={(|2-3{|}^{6}+|-1-0{|}^{6}+|1-2{|}^{6})}^{1/6} \\\\  & ={3}^{1/6} \\\\  & ≈1.201.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517996 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518221 1069 "q-163644" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518221 1071 "![](images/16/1664a86a1fee25cccbb710ad4335e4d69b36c24d64c95ae7bff9e54d2ac3a6b6.png)\n\nA plate with mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the curves $x=\\sqrt{2y}$ and $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}$, as shown above. Given that the region has an area of $\\frac{4}{3},$ find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $y=2.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518221 1072 #uuid "f58ffe9b-0496-58b1-afb0-490bd07410f5" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518221 1074 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{2y}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d9c529fe2d238a3d6ac4e20cfcd571f980c28a4eb2c13497fab3ccb0d15ac6eb.png)\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $y=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|y-2|.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{3M}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2y}}\\frac{3M}{4}|y-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2y}}(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-2)^{2}[x]_{x={y}^{2}/2}^{x=\\sqrt{2y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-2)^{2}(\\sqrt{2y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\sqrt{2}{y}^{5/2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}-4\\sqrt{2}{y}^{3/2}+2{y}^{3}+4\\sqrt{2}{y}^{1/2}-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{[\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}{y}^{7/2}}{7}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{10}-\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}{y}^{5/2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}{y}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}(\\frac{32}{7}-\\frac{32}{10}-\\frac{64}{5}+8+\\frac{32}{3}-\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}(\\frac{40}{21}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10M}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518029 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518222 1069 "q-277249" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518222 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt[4]{3x+15}-3.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518222 1072 #uuid "f9219fe2-5911-57bb-89ea-30d2a8256e49" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518222 1074 "To find the roots of $f(x),$ we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{3x+15}-3=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{3x+15}-3 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{3x+15} & =3 \\\\ (\\sqrt[4]{3x+15})^{4} & ={3}^{4} \\\\ 3x+15 & =81 \\\\ 3x & =66 \\\\ x & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{3\\cdot 22+15}-3 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{81}-3 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=22.$" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518051 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518223 1069 "q-60250" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518223 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing numbers in the following sentence?\n\nA student makes a mixture having $\\frac{4}{9}$ grams of ginger for each gram of glucose to make his own energy drink. Therefore, the energy drink has a ratio of $\\boxed{}$ grams of ginger to $\\boxed{}$ grams of glucose." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518223 1072 #uuid "fed5c231-48f8-530b-a66d-407d2c45633b" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518223 1074 "Let's rewrite the fraction $\\frac{4}{9}$ as a ratio:\n\n$$\n4:9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the energy drink has a ratio of $4$ grams of ginger to $9$ grams of glucose." 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518224 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518316 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518224 1062 #uuid "068d6b66-d519-5161-9eb3-7efaee833625" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518224 1063 #edb/ref 17592186362382 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518225 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518243 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518225 1062 #uuid "06956ae6-a0ed-51f5-858f-eee00b1ca616" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518225 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518189 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518226 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518303 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518226 1062 #uuid "09b16e88-2f98-5aaf-bffe-a62e02c9e116" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518226 1063 #edb/ref 17592186373285 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518227 1062 #uuid "0e3febc6-ffc1-5247-a54a-b79a2b1756e7" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518227 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518119 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518228 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518281 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518228 1062 #uuid "0e4a3401-c189-5f87-82f9-223689f4ee7b" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518228 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518126 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518229 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518230 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] [17592186518229 1062 #uuid "15d3249d-d1cd-53bd-a1e4-e6f226addfa8" 13194139535568 17592186045417 true] 